{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdYtGsVMFuk1Zma5g2UK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Radiation can flip bits in a chip's memory and corrupt data without triggering typical monitoring tools, and that is one of the main things Google's new Suncatcher satellite is meant to measure. The prototype launched this month on a Falcon 9 and is now online, carrying four TPUs, of which it can run only one at a time, for about 20 minutes, on its 1 kW of power. Google engineers sent its first prompt this week, asking the preloaded Gemma 4 model for a haiku about space and asking the spacecraft to report on the orbital conditions around it. A Google spokesperson told TechCrunch the satellite is operating nominally.\n\nA readable haiku shows the satellite can run the model. It says little about how often errors occur, which is the reason for the longer test campaign on thermal stress and radiation effects. Google has tested its TPUs extensively in particle accelerators on Earth, and that testing suggested radiation would have minimal impact on inference workloads and be more of a problem for training a model. The prototype lets the team assess those dynamics in actual orbit, where the article says they have so far worked from ground tests and simulation.\n\nThe distinction between the two workloads matters because Suncatcher is intended to lead to networks of close-flying satellites performing large inference in orbit. If the ground-test suggestion holds, that target would face a smaller radiation problem than training would. The orbital results will show whether it holds, and the article does not say what Google would change if the chips behave differently than the accelerator tests predicted.\n\nWhat follows depends on those results. If the TPUs prove capable, Google plans to launch two more satellites with the advanced chips in 2027, with higher power generation and inter-satellite optical links. A failure mode that standard monitoring may not catch makes the radiation data from this one-chip-at-a-time prototype the evidence those later satellites would rest on."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1259,"cache_read_input_tokens":7314,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1259},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ea27f4b-c52f-4580-a7f5-b3a6b7a08186","uuid":"b2f8dbde-9012-4b60-9c7a-824e67e720dd","timestamp":"2026-11-02T05:29:33.116Z","request_id":"req_011CgdYtGe6jBo5pGS3X29WC"}
